From: François Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: S-R deadline work, guard and
 inhibit

The S-R mode needs a way back to software that does not depend on
userspace, a stop that wins over an arm it overlaps, and a disarm that
works while RTNL or the parameter lock is held for long. This patch adds
them to the mode engine and its kernel binding; the control files that
drive them come next.

A delayed work keeps the PPE's deadline (WP3-DESIGN D7). While the mode
is READY or ACTIVE it takes the offload lock only, checks that the
rtl8_4s switch is in step with it, then the deadline
(ppe_sr_eng_tick(), pure): a switch that left READY or is gone disarms
(SWITCH_NOT_READY) as the statistics-time check would, a deadline reached
disarms (DEADLINE), a deadline found past the switch's own less 30 s is
clamped to it. Otherwise it comes back after the time left, 30 s at
most, so that a switch out of step is noticed with no traffic. An arm and
a renewal kick it; once the mode is off or in FAULT it is cancelled, and
the offload's stop waits for it.

A control disarm and the guard inhibit arms before they take the lock
(D8): an arm refuses with EPERM (INHIBIT) once it sees the inhibit, after
a barrier, under the lock. Only an uninhibit naming the inhibit's
generation lifts it (ESTALE otherwise), so a stop that overlaps an enable
wins and only an enable started after it can undo it. The flag and its
generation share one atomic word, so that an uninhibit and a concurrent
inhibit cannot leave the generation bumped with the flag clear.

The guard (D9) inhibits, then waits for the lock 2 s at most by the
clock, not by a count of 10 ms sleeps, which lasts twice as long at HZ
100, and disarms (GUARD); it takes neither the parameter lock nor RTNL.
The reboot notifier shares the bounded wait.

From before the arm's claim until the mode is off again, the PPE holds a
reference on the module of the switch's driver (D16): rtl8372n_dsa cannot
be unloaded while S-R may be live in hardware, FAULT included. The arm
also refuses while the system is not running (ESHUTDOWN), returns the
offers step's error rather than dropping it, and refuses an arm without
a deadline (EINVAL) or with less than 60 s left (ERANGE) with the new
reason DEADLINE. A scope swap that leaves a flow behind returns EIO; the
last transition records the flows it could not drop, offers off
included; the status gains the inhibit, the module reference, the
switch's deadline, the scope, the renewals and the control and guard
counters, and keeps the PPE's deadline for READY and ACTIVE only; the
idle verify ends with a verdict line.

Nothing calls the new controls yet.

Signed-off-by: François Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
